  "scope_and_limits": [
    "Two separate processes, each with 500000 generated files, three full mounted traversals and a new file revision before each subsequent pass.",
    "single: one 500000-file directory; many: 500 directories of 1000 files. Metadata was indexed in pages through the normal refresh path. No cloud credentials or cloud data.",
    "Each first_entry sample holds the first directory open; seconds measure opening and fetching its first entry. Closed and invalidated samples measure the full traversal from its start. Revision refresh time is not included in traversal seconds.",
    "The fixture asserts snapshot reservations return to zero after final deferred close and only the root view remains after normal kernel invalidation/reclamation. No lookup references are fabricated or discarded.",
    "Logical snapshot bytes exclude physical block allocation, filesystem metadata, kernel page cache, SQLite and persistent inode history. Process RSS/PSS and high-water marks are separate measurements.",
    "Full directory construction still precedes its first entry. The giant-directory first-entry latency is 5.49 to 7.31 seconds and does not meet a 500ms navigation target.",
    "Post-invalidation RSS still increases across revision rounds in both variants. This run does not establish a memory plateau or close the namespace capacity gate.",
    "No combined large-library deep paths, aliases, held mappings, cold foreground publication, writable local overlays, desktop thumbnails, failure of kernel reply delivery, real-provider or 24-hour churn acceptance.",
    "This is an implementation measurement, not an old-versus-new binary performance comparison."
  ]
}
